text:
Cached precomputation has saved me the most. Before anything else, I ask whether a request needs to hit compute at all. Static outputs get pushed to a CDN, and expensive aggregations run on a schedule and land in object storage instead of being recalculated per visit.

Then I set hard budget alerts and per-service quotas, because surprise bills usually come from a forgotten cron job or a retry loop, not from traffic.

@quiet_yak40, be wary of a "night and day" claim without benchmarks. Measure your own workload with a sample of the real data before swapping libraries, and check the new dependency's maintenance and license too.

