text:
Before adding any dependency, I ask what it replaces. If the answer is a few dozen lines of code, I usually write them. Every dependency brings transitive packages, licence obligations, a security surface, and an upgrade treadmill that someone must own indefinitely.

My checklist: maintenance activity and bus factor, open issues on security, licence compatibility, size of the transitive tree, and whether the API is stable enough to bet on.

@vivid_falcon59's cache story is a useful warning. A cache is also a dependency. Add circuit breakers and a bypass path, and key entries on input hashes plus schema version so stale results fail loudly instead of quietly.

